WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.990
Pixar is famous for dropping Easter eggs throughout
their films, and the Toy Story franchise's

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:11.710
fourth installment is full of tiny details
only the most eagle-eyed viewers will catch.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:15.680
Here's a list of things you probably missed
in Toy Story 4.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:21.240
Toy Story 4 starts with a flashback to nine
years earlier, where Woody and Bo Peep are

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:26.589
conducting a rescue mission to save Andy's
remote control toy car, RC, from being swept

00:00:26.589 --> 00:00:27.920
away in a rainstorm.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:34.130
Luckily, the team is able to save RC just
as a real car pulls into the Davis' driveway.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:39.410
This crucial scene reveals where in the world
Bo Peep was in Toy Story 3, because the person

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:42.070
driving the car is there to take her away.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:46.480
If tears aren't clouding your eyes, you may
be able to make out the letters and numbers

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.450
on the car's license plate: RMR F97.

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:55.770
In a 2019 press conference, producer Mark
Nielsen revealed that those letters and digits

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:57.800
were fully intentional, saying:

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:02.690
"On Toy Story 2, there was someone who worked
on the show that accidentally deleted the

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:04.150
movie from the render farm.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:08.000
[That] was the command on the computer that
deleted the film."

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.539
Producer Jonas Rivera added:

00:01:09.539 --> 00:01:13.939
"It felt appropriate that the license plate
on the car that takes Bo away would be the

00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:16.600
same that killed Toy Story 2."

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.920
Randy Newman's cheery song, "You've Got a
Friend In Me," was the theme song for the

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:25.609
very first Toy Story movie, and has since
been used in each of its sequels.

00:01:25.609 --> 00:01:30.759
The famous song made a welcome comeback with
Toy Story 4, but the film also graced fans

00:01:30.759 --> 00:01:34.070
with two brand spankin' new Randy Newman tunes.

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:38.369
The first of said tunes, "I Can't Let You
Throw Yourself Away," can be heard during

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:42.990
the montage of Woody quite literally not letting
Forky throw himself away.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:44.659
"You're Bonnie's toy.

00:01:44.659 --> 00:01:49.400
You are going to help create happy memories
that will last for the rest of her life."

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:50.640
"Huh, what?"

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.320
"Oh, ah, ugh!"

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.009
A more appropriate song title has never existed.

00:01:56.009 --> 00:01:57.210
As Newman explained:

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:01.969
"[Forky] gets it in his head that he's disposable,
so he keeps trying to throw himself in the

00:02:01.969 --> 00:02:06.459
trash can … and Woody has put himself in
charge of keeping him from doing so."

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:10.920
The second of the singer-songwriter's new
original songs is the backing track for the

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:15.310
film's end credits — though Newman gave
his voice a break for this one.

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:20.260
Instead, country crooner Chris Stapleton lent
his vocal talent to the Western-inspired tune,

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:23.360
titled, "The Ballad of the Lonesome Cowboy."

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:28.680
Though fans had long speculated that Pixar
films were all connected in some way, writer

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:35.260
Jon Negroni popularized the idea with a viral
2013 blog post titled "The Pixar Theory."

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:40.310
In his post, Negroni put forth the idea that
Boo — the adorable little girl from Monsters,

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:42.670
Inc. — is actually the witch from Brave.

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:47.790
You know, the witch who transformed Brave's
villain Mor'du from a prince to a bear.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:49.040
As Negroni explained:

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.820
"She probably wanted to turn him into a bear
because [Sulley] resembles a bear, and she

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:57.150
is still trying to figure out where [Sulley]
comes from."

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:01.340
As outlandish as it may sound, there seem
to be a couple of Easter eggs hidden throughout

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:06.270
Toy Story 4 that will make the hearts of Pixar
Theory believers soar.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:10.550
During Bonnie's first day of Kindergarten,
an out-of-focus Boo seemingly sits just a

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:15.530
few tables away — but the more obvious egg
comes in the form of Bunny, one of the stuffed

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:17.910
prizes Buzz meets at the carnival.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:22.160
As Entertainment Weekly notes, the fuzzy,
floppy-eared, blue-and-green Bunny bears a

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:25.040
striking resemblance to Sulley the monster.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.040
Here's hoping Boo's family makes a trip to
the carnival!

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:33.600
When Toy Story 3 was released in June 2010,
fans were appalled to see Bo Peep, as well

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:37.680
as her beloved sheep, Billy, Goat, and Gruff,
had seemingly been erased.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.690
Fortunately, the fourth installment of the
franchise made it up to fans in a major way

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:47.770
by putting Bo and her journey to be more than
just a toy collecting dust on a shelf front-and-center.

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:53.230
"Woody, who needs a kid's room when you can
have all of this?"

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:56.170
In a press conference, director Josh Cooley
revealed:

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:59.370
"Having Bo come back was the genesis of this
film.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:02.510
We even called the movie 'Peep," which was
our code name."

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:08.000
The fan favorite's return was announced in
January 2019 with a short video of Bo startling

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.880
Buzz and Woody by bursting through a wall
behind them, wielding her trademark crook.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:17.639
However, much to the surprise of fans, her
signature pink dress and bonnet were nowhere

00:04:17.639 --> 00:04:18.659
to be found.

00:04:18.659 --> 00:04:23.349
Instead, the new-and-improved Bo sports a
baby-blue jumpsuit with ruffled accents and

00:04:23.349 --> 00:04:27.120
cap sleeves, along with a pink sash tied around
her waist.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:31.770
If you found yourself wishing you, too, could
sport Bo's new look — we have good news.

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:37.479
ShopDisney.com has the entire look available
for just $168.

